ritation"},{"code":"H319","text":"Causes serious eye irritation"},{"code":"H332","text":"Harmful if inhaled"},{"code":"H361d","text":"Suspected of damaging the unborn child"},{"code":"H372","text":"Causes damage to organs through prolonged or repeated exposure"}],"severe":false,"flash_point_C":30,"autoignition_C":490,"LFL_pct":1.0,"UFL_pct":8.0,"Tb_C":145},{"id":"H2O","name":"Water","formula":"H2O","cas":"7732-18-5","ghs":[],"severe":false,"flash_point_C":null,"autoignition_C":null,"LFL_pct":null,"UFL_pct":23.5,"Tb_C":100}],"hazop":[{"node":"Reactor and furnace","deviation":"Air ingress under vacuum","cause":"Flange or expansion joint leak","consequence":"Hydrogen rich flammable mixture in hot equipment","safeguard":"Oxygen analysers on the off gas, steam purge, trips"},{"node":"Superheater","deviation":"Loss of steam flow","cause":"Steam drum level or control valve fault","consequence":"Tube overheating and rupture","safeguard":"Low flow trip on the fuel, tube skin thermocouples"},{"node":"EB/styrene splitter","deviation":"High temperature","cause":"Loss of vacuum or inhibitor","consequence":"Styrene polymerises in the column, fouling or a runaway","safeguard":"Inhibitor dosing, temperature trips, vacuum alarms"},{"node":"Styrene storage","deviation":"High temperature","cause":"Chiller failure in hot weather","consequence":"Self heating runaway polymerisation","safeguard":"Chilled storage below 20 C, TBC inhibitor with dissolved oxygen checks, short pinch storage"}],"process_hazards":[{"hazard":"High temperature","detail":"Up to 800 C. Creep, hydrogen attack and burns; refractory and tube skin temperature monitoring."},{"hazard":"Flammable materials","detail":"Benzene, Toluene, Ethylbenzene, Ethylene, Hydrogen, Methane, Styrene. Hazardous area classification, ignition source control, gas detection, fire protection."}],"validation":[{"metric":"Styrene yield from EB dehydrogenation","model":94.0,"published":[88,94],"unit":"%","error_pct":0.0,"inside_range":true,"source":"Styrene, Wikipedia (citing Catalysis Reviews 1974)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Styrene","note":"Model selectivity on ethylbenzene converted.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":0,"pass":true},{"metric":"Ethylbenzene per lb styrene","model":1.084421,"published":1.085,"unit":"lb/lb","error_pct":-0.053367,"inside_range":null,"source":"US DOE, Energy and Environmental Profile of the US Chemical Industry (2000), Table 4-6","url":"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1218625","note":"DOE footnote d, from Brown 1996. Unreacted ethylbenzene is recycled, so this is the net use.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":3,"pass":true},{"metric":"Heat of combustion of the ethylbenzene feed","model":20044.707972,"published":20058,"unit":"Btu/lb styrene","error_pct":-0.066268,"inside_range":null,"source":"US DOE, Energy and Environmental Profile of the US Chemical Industry (2000), Table 4-6","url":"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1218625","note":"Model uses the gross (higher) heat of combustion of ethylbenzene.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":3,"pass":true},{"metric":"Net process energy","model":5562.911997,"published":[14370,19412],"unit":"Btu/lb styrene","error_pct":-61.288017,"inside_range":false,"source":"US DOE, Energy and Environmental Profile of the US Chemical Industry (2000), Table 4-6","url":"https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1218625","note":"US 1997 range: furnace fuel plus fuel to raise the steam. The model reads low because it assumes modern heat recovery (waste heat boiler raises the dilution steam, off gas fires the furnace) that many 1990s US plants did not have.","kind":"process","tolerance_pct":20,"pass":false}],"assumptions":["Selectivity 94% to styrene; the losses are split 3.5% to benzene and ethylene and 2.5% to toluene and methane. Tar and coke make are not modelled.","Per pass conversion 65% over two beds at 620 C inlet; all unreacted ethylbenzene is recovered in the splitter and recycled.","Steam to oil ratio 1.6 kg/kg, raised from stripped process condensate in a waste heat boiler on the effluent, with LP steam topping up any shortfall.","The hydrogen rich off gas is compressed and burnt in the superheater; natural gas covers the balance.","Benzene and toluene are burnt in the superheater; in an integrated complex they go back to the ethylbenzene unit.","Condensate reuse 90%, with the blowdown sent to treatment."],"notes":[]}